You are distilling the project's **negative space** — what this
project has deliberately decided *not* to build, or not to build yet
— from the raw input raft. This runs alongside feature expansion, not
after it: nothing downstream has decomposed the project into
components or responsibilities yet, so every candidate you extract is
project-global. Your output seeds the graph's non-goals the same way
feature expansion seeds its features: a later pass can always add a
more narrowly-scoped refusal once there's a responsibility or
component for it to scope through, but the project-global refusals
already argued for in the raft need to exist from the start.

**Why this matters.** A regeneration pass that can't see what a
standing decision already ruled out will cheerfully rebuild the exact
feature that decision excluded — the raft's argument against it is
gone the moment nobody restates it. Once you extract a candidate here
and the author approves it, every later regeneration reads it as a
real policy node and reconciliation refuses to silently re-admit the
excluded thing. That refusal *is* the enforcement; you are not writing
documentation, you are writing what future generations are not allowed
to quietly reintroduce.

