# What counts as a candidate

A candidate is anything the raft argues, explicitly or implicitly,
that this project will **not** build, or will not build **yet**:

* An explicit refusal: "we will not support multi-tenancy", "no mobile
  app in v1", "this does not replace the existing billing system".
* An implicit refusal that falls out of a stated constraint: a raft
  that commits to "single-writer, single-region" is implicitly
  refusing multi-writer and multi-region, even if it never uses the
  word "not".
* An argued deferral: something the raft says is out of scope *for
  now*, with either no stated return condition ("never, argued" — the
  raft gives a permanent reason) or a concrete trigger ("not until we
  have more than one paying customer", "revisit once usage data
  exists").

**What is not a candidate**: a feature the raft simply never mentions.
Silence is not a non-goal — flagging every unmentioned capability as
"not building X" would produce noise indistinguishable from the real,
argued exclusions this tier exists to protect. Extract only what the
raft actually argues against, explicitly or by a stated constraint's
clear implication.

# Output format

Emit two top-level blocks in this order: `<introduction>` and
`<candidates>`. The `<introduction>` is optional — a short prose
preamble (1-3 paragraphs) capturing anything about how you read the
raft's negative space worth a future regeneration knowing: where the
raft argues loosely and you had to infer, where it's explicit and
unambiguous.

After it, emit `<candidates>`, zero or more `<candidate>` children —
zero when the raft genuinely argues no exclusions, which is legal and
should not be padded with speculative candidates.

    <introduction>
      The raft is explicit about no multi-tenancy and no mobile
      client in v1. It also commits to a single Postgres instance
      with no read replicas, which implicitly rules out geographic
      distribution until that constraint is revisited.
    </introduction>
    <candidates>
      <candidate>
        <name>No Multi-Tenancy</name>
        <trigger>any schema or query design implying per-tenant isolation</trigger>
        <rationale>The raft states every deployment serves exactly one organization; building tenant isolation now is unrequested complexity with no current customer.</rationale>
        <revisit-condition>never, argued</revisit-condition>
      </candidate>
      <candidate>
        <name>No Read Replicas</name>
        <trigger>any component design assuming more than one database instance</trigger>
        <rationale>The raft commits to a single Postgres instance for the initial deployment to keep the operational surface small while the project is unproven.</rationale>
        <revisit-condition>not until sustained read load or availability requirements make a single instance the bottleneck</revisit-condition>
      </candidate>
    </candidates>

# Rules

* Emit **exactly one** `<candidates>` root-level block (it may be
  empty). Nothing before `<introduction>`, nothing after
  `<candidates>`.
* Each `<candidate>` has exactly one `<name>`, one `<trigger>`, one
  `<rationale>`, and — always fill this, since every candidate here is
  by definition an argued refusal or deferral — one
  `<revisit-condition>`.
* `<name>` is a short identifier, title case, naming what is being
  excluded — "No Multi-Tenancy", not "Tenancy Policy".
* `<trigger>` is a short semantic phrase naming what a later pass
  would be doing when it's about to reintroduce the excluded thing —
  the same role a sysarch `<policy>`'s `<trigger>` plays, phrased so a
  downstream architecture or reconciliation pass recognizes the
  situation this candidate is guarding against.
* `<rationale>` states the raft's own argument for the exclusion, not
  your own opinion of whether it's a good idea. Ground it in what the
  raft actually says or clearly implies; don't invent a stronger
  argument than the raft makes.
* `<revisit-condition>` is free text. Write `never, argued` when the
  raft gives no return condition — the exclusion is permanent as far
  as the raft's own argument goes — or a concrete condition ("not
  until X") when the raft frames it as a deferral rather than a
  permanent refusal. These are the same field with two kinds of value,
  not two different shapes.
* Do not fabricate exclusions the raft doesn't argue for. An empty
  `<candidates></candidates>` block is the correct output for a raft
  that states no exclusions, explicit or implicit.
* Do not include meta-commentary about what you are doing or how you
  arrived at the list. Output only `<introduction>` (if included) and
  `<candidates>`.
